# 1bit-console - what /etc/inittab respawns on tty1.
#
# Puppy's inittab says `tty1::respawn:plogin`, and plogin does a lot before its last line: it reads
# /etc/plogin, sources /etc/rc.d/PUPSTATE, lays out /root and a user's home, rewrites invalid symlinks, then
# `exec getty -n -l /bin/autologin 115200,38400,9600 $TTY`. On the 2026-11-02 board boot (cycle 13, headless
# entry) that chain left **no prompt on tty1** - measured over ssh: `getty 38400 tty2` running and nothing on
# tty1 - while the same image gave a prompt on tty1 in QEMU. A console-only machine whose console has nothing
# to log into is not headless, it is unreachable, so the appliance's own console cannot depend on that chain.
#
# On a headless boot (pfix=nox) this is plogin's last step and nothing else: busybox init already opened the
# tty for us, getty with -n skips the login banner and -l /bin/autologin logs root in, which is what the rows
# and the tty1 reader (1bit-tty1) expect. Every other boot still runs plogin, because it is what lays out the
# first X session on the desktop entry.
# The "Install 1bit OS to a disk" entry (owner, cycle 14): tty1 IS the installer there. It is done here
# rather than by a service of its own so nothing else competes for the same terminal - a getty and the
# installer both reading tty1 would eat each other's keystrokes.
if grep -q '1bit.installer=1' /proc/cmdline 2> /dev/null; then
    exec /usr/local/bin/1bit-console-install
fi
if ! grep -q 'pfix=[^ ]*nox' /proc/cmdline 2> /dev/null; then
    exec /bin/plogin
fi
exec getty -n -l /bin/autologin 115200,38400,9600 tty1
